he denominator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the denominator as difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{2\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-119869/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-119869"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65182"] :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the denominator as difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-65293/selection/81d31a2e82f9c3ee5b2bc61d0410bfbabe8d187f8c5fc792d56fa77ce9aecc9a" :answer/id #uuid "c8ea53a8-1e46-54d4-b4e2-c5eb07a7aa74" :answer/type :answer.t